{
  "dom_id": "step-q76644",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191213/assets/fb5fddbf843a89ffbefeaed1976a2bfd159fea9a41665f99aee260f110016e1c.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{OE}$ are the bisectors of $∠AOC$ and $∠BOC,$ respectively. If $m∠DOC=x,$ $m∠COE=2x+{2}^{∘},$ and $m∠AOB={130}^{∘},$ find the value of $m∠BOC.$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191213/assets/484a70ad0c9eeca1ea03db65f5c687074189ac93f9894b64598278dc285d0d83.png)\n\nFirst, let's express the measures of $∠AOC$ and $∠BOC,$ in terms of $x.$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\to }{OD}$ is the bisector of $∠AOC,$ we have\n\n$$\nm∠AOC=2m∠DOC=2x.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\to }{OE}$ is the bisector of $∠BOC,$ we have\n\n$$\nm∠BOC=2m∠COE=4x+{4}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSince $m∠AOB={130}^{∘},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC+m∠BOC & =m∠AOB \\\\ 2x+4x+{4}^{∘} & ={130}^{∘} \\\\ 6x+{4}^{∘} & ={130}^{∘} \\\\ 6x & ={126}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={21}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are asked to find $m∠BOC.$ Substituting $x={21}^{∘}$ into the expression for $m∠BOC$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOC & =4({21}^{∘})+{4}^{∘} \\\\  & ={84}^{∘}+{4}^{∘} \\\\  & ={88}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
